<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shulü Ping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/879">879</a>–953</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Empress Yingtian, Empress Shulü</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shulü Ping was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, and a powerful figure in its early history. After his death in 926 she secured the throne for her second son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Yelü Deguang</a>, over the eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a>, and according to tradition cut off her own hand to place in her husband's tomb. She was later defeated and confined by Shizong.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shulü Ping was born on 19 October 879, and her nickname was Yueliduo. Her great-great-grandfather Shulü Nuosi was said to be of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> descent, and her father, Shulü Pogu, served under the Yaonian clan of the Khitan. Her mother was said to be a daughter of a King Yundejia, apparently Yelü Yundeshi, the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Yelü Abaoji</a>, which would have made her and her husband cousins. When they married is not known. She bore him three sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Yelü Deguang</a> and Yelü Lihu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a> declared himself emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in 916, he made her empress. She was described as brave, resolute and full of strategies and took part in his military decisions. Left in charge of the headquarters while he campaigned against the Dangxiang, she ambushed and crushed a raid by two Shiwei tribes, the Huangtou and the Choubo. She opposed the campaign of 921 against Jin, which ended in a Khitan defeat, while the conquest of Balhae in 926 was said to have followed her strategies.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>'s death she had many generals and officials she considered hard to control killed, and she steered the assembled chieftains to choose her favourite son, who became Emperor Taizong. As empress dowager she was said to make the key decisions of state. She married Taizong to her niece Xiao Wen, appointed the Han official Han Yanhui as a chancellor, and in 945 opposed the war against Later Jin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Taizong died on his way north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, she wanted Yelü Lihu to succeed him, but the Khitan chieftains, remembering her killings, backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shizong-of-liao">Yelü Ruan</a>, who declared himself emperor as Shizong. Her troops under Lihu were defeated by Yelü Anduan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-liuge">Yelü Liuge</a>, and she accepted Shizong in a peace agreement. He placed her under house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>'s tomb in Zu Prefecture, where she died on 1 August 953, in the reign of Emperor Muzong. She was buried with her husband.</p><p>She is credited with changing what was expected of widows in Khitan society, in which women had been expected to sacrifice themselves: by not doing so she set an example, at least for women of the elite. Unlike the Han empress dowagers of the time, who were expected to wield influence behind the scenes, she exercised her power openly at the courts of both her husband and her son.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shulü Ping” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His widow, the <b>Empress Shulü</b> Ping, secured the throne for their second son, Yelü Deguang (Emperor Taizong, r. 927–947), who in 936 helped Shi Jingtang found the Later Jin dynasty in north China and received in return the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing and Datong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
